WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.573
Even with a full back room, the corner shop has a morning routine.

2
00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:10.329
Wheel out the shelves, switch on the tills, and get up to speed.

3
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:15.818
Now picture a pop up stall, built at a factory, fully stocked, and loaded onto a van.

4
00:00:15.818 --> 00:00:19.175
Park it, open the shutter, and you're trading at once.

5
00:00:19.175 --> 00:00:23.652
The catch is printed on the side: it sells only what the factory packed.

6
00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:27.304
Here's the question you might be shy to ask.

7
00:00:27.304 --> 00:00:31.721
If a native image starts in milliseconds, why doesn't everyone use one?

8
00:00:31.721 --> 00:00:32.779
What's the catch?

9
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:37.568
Lesson 2's best tool still started a JVM.

10
00:00:37.568 --> 00:00:50.840
In a benchmark from Quarkus, a framework for Java services, the first request took 4,417 milliseconds on a plain JVM, and 1,859 with the AOT cache.

11
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:56.494
GraalVM's native image builds the stall.

12
00:00:56.494 --> 00:01:08.425
It turns your program, and the parts of the JDK it uses, into one ready to run file for one operating system and one kind of CPU, with no JVM to start.

13
00:01:08.425 --> 00:01:13.087
It comes with GraalVM or Mandrel, not with a plain OpenJDK.

14
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.613
So how does the factory know what to pack?

15
00:01:16.613 --> 00:01:18.852
It follows the shop's own catalogue.

16
00:01:18.852 --> 00:01:23.642
It starts at main, and follows every method call and field access it can see.

17
00:01:23.642 --> 00:01:26.379
Every item a page points to goes in the van.

18
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.488
That's step one, the analysis.

19
00:01:28.488 --> 00:01:37.992
Step two, it runs static initializers where it can, and saves their objects in the image heap, a part of the file mapped straight into memory at start.

20
00:01:37.992 --> 00:01:41.831
Step three, it compiles every reached method to machine code.

21
00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:47.593
Code the analysis never reached is left out, and so are the JDK classes nothing uses.

22
00:01:47.593 --> 00:01:54.591
That's called the closed world assumption: everything the program will ever run is known when you build it.

23
00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:03.585
The finished file runs on a small runtime, Substrate VM: no class loading, no bytecode checks, no interpreter, and no JIT.

24
00:02:03.585 --> 00:02:07.097
That's why it starts in milliseconds and stays small.

25
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.343
Here's the benchmark.

26
00:02:10.343 --> 00:02:21.723
Time to first request: 4,417 milliseconds on the JVM, 1,859 with the cache, and 581 as a native image.

27
00:02:21.723 --> 00:02:30.313
Memory, as RSS, the memory the operating system counts: 304 MiB, 240, and 95.

28
00:02:30.533 --> 00:02:38.119
Now the last row: peak throughput, the most requests per second the service handles once it's busy.

29
00:02:38.119 --> 00:02:39.882
The JVM managed 13,265.

30
00:02:39.882 --> 00:02:44.096
The native image was compiled completely before it ran.

31
00:02:44.096 --> 00:02:46.471
More, about the same, or fewer?

32
00:02:46.471 --> 00:02:47.697
Pause and guess.

33
00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:50.959
Fewer.

34
00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:53.960
5,411 requests per second.

35
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.729
That's 59 percent lower.

36
00:02:56.933 --> 00:03:01.646
A JIT compiles while the program runs, guided by profiles of real traffic.

37
00:03:01.646 --> 00:03:05.913
The build has no traffic to watch, so its code is less specialized.

38
00:03:05.913 --> 00:03:10.308
The stall's staff follow a printed script, and never learn shortcuts.

39
00:03:11.233 --> 00:03:18.291
So a native image is a sprinter who never trains: brilliant off the line, and no faster on the tenth lap.

40
00:03:18.291 --> 00:03:22.660
The JVM starts slowly, learns the course, and wins the long race.

41
00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:27.140
Jokes aside: native wins start-up and memory, not peak speed.

42
00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:31.224
There's a second price, and it's the one that bites.

43
00:03:31.224 --> 00:03:35.778
Reflection is code that looks up classes and methods by name while it runs.

44
00:03:35.778 --> 00:03:39.543
If the name is written in the code, the analysis works it out.

45
00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:47.156
But the tracking service reads messages from its depots, and each one names its event class in a header, at run time.

46
00:03:47.156 --> 00:03:49.999
The analysis is clever, but it isn't psychic.

47
00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:56.514
In the shop, that's a customer with a note in their pocket: do you have the thing my aunt wrote down?

48
00:03:56.514 --> 00:03:59.404
The stall can sell it only if the factory packed it.

49
00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:03.801
On a normal JVM, lookups by name always work.

50
00:04:03.801 --> 00:04:09.042
The lesson's DynamicLookupDemo calls a method chosen by two strings.

51
00:04:09.042 --> 00:04:16.055
With LocalDate and now, it printed today's date, and with UUID and randomUUID, a random id.

52
00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:17.923
Now think like the factory.

53
00:04:17.923 --> 00:04:23.199
To support every possible pair of strings, which classes would the build have to pack?

54
00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:24.180
Pause and think.

55
00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:28.818
All of them: the whole warehouse.

56
00:04:28.818 --> 00:04:37.207
So the build packs only what it can see, and a name it was never told about fails at run time, with MissingReflectionRegistrationError.

57
00:04:37.207 --> 00:04:39.693
That's an Error, not meant to be caught.

58
00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.915
Here the stall picture breaks, and it costs real time.

59
00:04:43.915 --> 00:04:46.799
A new price tag on a real stall takes a minute.

60
00:04:46.799 --> 00:04:52.631
One changed line of your code means building the whole stall again, at the factory, every time.

61
00:04:53.533 --> 00:04:54.820
So here's the rule.

62
00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:58.204
A native image runs only what the build could see.

63
00:04:58.204 --> 00:05:01.657
Anything your code finds by name must be on a list.

64
00:05:01.657 --> 00:05:04.771
GraalVM calls that list reachability metadata.

65
00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:07.817
The demo even prints the entry it would need.

66
00:05:08.733 --> 00:05:10.444
So when is native worth it?

67
00:05:10.444 --> 00:05:14.057
When start-up time or memory per copy is your real limit.

68
00:05:14.057 --> 00:05:21.788
Not when you need the best peak speed, and not when your code finds classes by names from data, unless you keep that list.

69
00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:23.973
So, what's the catch?

70
00:05:23.973 --> 00:05:26.701
The stall sells only what the factory packed.

71
00:05:26.701 --> 00:05:29.975
You get a start in milliseconds and a small footprint.

72
00:05:29.975 --> 00:05:35.249
You give up the JIT's peak speed, quick builds, and every class the build couldn't see.

73
00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:36.259
Quick check.

74
00:05:36.259 --> 00:05:43.125
Your code calls Class.forName with a class name read from a settings file, and nobody listed that class.

75
00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:45.172
What happens in a native image?

76
00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:51.110
It fails at run time, with a missing registration error.

77
00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:54.692
The build never saw the name, so the class was never packed.
